Both pointers are built from the same raw pointer. Each starts its own count at 1.

l9 gave the rule: prefer `unique_ptr`. Here is what each class is.

```cpp
// Say aloud: unique owns one thing and moves;
// shared counts its owners and the last one frees.
// unique: one owner, move only, no overhead
// shared: a count, copy is cheap, last owner frees
```

